# Chavruta.AI — REGRESSION eval v1: questions that FAILED in production, with what should surface.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
#   The other datasets here were written from the outside in — "what should a Torah RAG answer?".
#   This one is written from the inside out: every row is a real failure a real user hit, with the
#   date it was caught. Those are the most valuable eval items there are, because they are the only
#   ones already proven to be reachable. A generic set tells you how good the system is; this set
#   tells you whether a bug you already paid for came back.
#
#   It is also, deliberately, the seed of any future RL training set (docs/REINFORCEMENT_LEARNING.md):
#   the reward signal for retrieval — "did the right source surface?" — is exactly what each row
#   encodes, and these rows need no human labelling because production already labelled them.
#
# FORMAT  {"qid","question","lang","expected_refs":[...],"intent","note","history":[...]}
#   expected_refs  segment-prefix matched by the harness, so a topic-level anchor
#                  ("Shulchan_Arukh,_Orach_Chayim.310") matches any segment under it.
#   history        prior USER turns, oldest first — for follow-ups that carry no topic of their own.
#   expected_refs == []  ⇒  honest no-source must hold.
#
# RUN   python scripts/run_eval.py --dataset eval/regressions_v1.jsonl --retrieval-only
#
{"qid": "reg-modern-01", "question": "האם מותר לשחק במחשב בשבת", "lang": "he", "expected_refs": ["Shulchan_Arukh,_Orach_Chayim.310", "B'Mareh_HaBazak"], "intent": "qa", "note": "2026-08-07: a modern device must reach the electricity/muktzeh sources, not the שחוק sugya (Avodah Zarah 56) it shares a root with. Fixed by the router's tech-concept bridge."}
{"qid": "reg-modern-02", "question": "האם מותר להשתמש בחשמל בשבת", "lang": "he", "expected_refs": ["B'Mareh_HaBazak"], "intent": "qa", "note": "2026-08-07 control case: the SAME halachic question phrased directly always worked. If this passes while reg-modern-01 fails, the gap is query understanding, not the corpus."}
{"qid": "reg-modern-03", "question": "מותר לדבר בטלפון בשבת?", "lang": "he", "expected_refs": ["Shulchan_Arukh,_Orach_Chayim.310", "B'Mareh_HaBazak"], "intent": "qa", "note": "same class as reg-modern-01, different device — guards the fix generalising past the one word that was caught."}
{"qid": "reg-followup-01", "question": "תנסה", "lang": "he", "expected_refs": ["Middot", "Sukkah", "Shekalim"], "intent": "qa", "history": ["מה המחלוקת בין רש\"י לתוספות לגבי בניית בית המקדש ואיך אפשר ליישב אותה"], "note": "2026-08-11: a bare follow-up retrieved on the literal word and returned sources about the CONCEPT of a test (Job 4:2, the Akeidah). It must retrieve on the topic it is following up on."}
{"qid": "reg-followup-02", "question": "למה?", "lang": "he", "expected_refs": ["Shulchan_Arukh,_Orach_Chayim.310"], "intent": "qa", "history": ["האם מותר לטלטל מחשב בשבת"], "note": "same class as reg-followup-01 — the shortest possible follow-up, which carries no retrievable content whatsoever."}
{"qid": "reg-followup-03", "question": "עכשיו שאלה אחרת לגמרי — מה הדין של נטילת ידיים לסעודה", "lang": "he", "expected_refs": ["Shulchan_Arukh,_Orach_Chayim.158"], "intent": "qa", "note": "the NEGATIVE case for follow-up anchoring: a full question mid-conversation must retrieve on its own topic, not be dragged back to the previous one."}
{"qid": "reg-commentator-01", "question": "מה אומר רש\"י על בניית בית המקדש לעתיד לבוא", "lang": "he", "expected_refs": ["Rashi_on_Sukkah", "Sukkah.41", "Rashi_on_Rosh_Hashanah"], "intent": "explain", "note": "2026-08-11: a thematic question naming a commentator answered 'no Rashi in the corpus' because round one scored other material higher and the self-fetch loop only ran on a COMPLETELY empty retrieval."}
{"qid": "reg-outofcorpus-01", "question": "מה פוסק הרב עובדיה יוסף על שימוש במדיח כלים בשבת", "lang": "he", "expected_refs": [], "intent": "qa", "note": "honesty guard: Yabia Omer is not in the commercial corpus. Naming a posek we do not hold must yield an honest no-source, never a similar-sounding substitute (Principle I)."}
#
# ---- 2026-08-13: patterns harvested from production failures (scripts/harvest_failed_questions.py)
# The QUESTIONS BELOW ARE WRITTEN FRESH. Real user text is never committed to this repository —
# reading it internally is permitted by the privacy policy, publishing it is not, and paraphrasing
# someone's sentence does not make it ours. What is reused is the RETRIEVAL PATTERN that failed.
# Every expected_ref below was verified to exist in chavruta_commercial before being written here.
{"qid": "reg-sugya-01", "question": "בוא נלמד את הסוגיה של שניים אוחזין בטלית", "lang": "he", "expected_refs": ["Bava_Metzia.3"], "intent": "chavruta", "note": "2026-08-13 pattern: a sugya named ONLY by its colloquial opening words, with no tractate and no daf. Returned no source. Talmud is amud-linear — Bava Metzia 2a is Bava_Metzia.3 (N=2*daf-1)."}
{"qid": "reg-sugya-02", "question": "הסבר את המחלוקת בין בית שמאי לבית הלל בהדלקת נר חנוכה", "lang": "he", "expected_refs": ["Shabbat.42", "Shulchan_Arukh,_Orach_Chayim.671"], "intent": "lesson", "note": "2026-08-13 pattern: a named machloket whose location (שבת כא ע\"ב) the asker does not give. Returned no source despite both the sugya and the halacha being in the corpus."}
{"qid": "reg-rambam-01", "question": "מה כותב הרמב\"ם בהלכות תלמוד תורה על חיוב הלימוד", "lang": "he", "expected_refs": ["Mishneh_Torah,_Torah_Study.1"], "intent": "qa", "note": "2026-08-13 pattern: a work named in Hebrew by its author plus the section name, no numbers. Returned no source; Mishneh_Torah,_Torah_Study.1.1 is in the corpus."}
{"qid": "reg-melech-01", "question": "בנה שיעור על מצוות מינוי מלך", "lang": "he", "expected_refs": ["Deuteronomy.17.14", "Deuteronomy.17.15", "Mishneh_Torah,_Kings_and_Wars.1"], "intent": "lesson", "note": "2026-08-13 pattern: a lesson request naming a mitzvah, where the base pasuk must anchor alongside the code. Returned no source."}
{"qid": "reg-aveida-01", "question": "מהי מהותה של מצוות השבת אבידה", "lang": "he", "expected_refs": ["Deuteronomy.22.1", "Shulchan_Arukh,_Choshen_Mishpat.259"], "intent": "lesson", "note": "2026-08-13 pattern: an abstract 'what is the essence of' framing of a concrete mitzvah. Asked three times in production in slightly different words, no source every time."}
{"qid": "reg-lashonhara-01", "question": "מהו המקור בתורה לאיסור לשון הרע", "lang": "he", "expected_refs": ["Leviticus.19.16"], "intent": "qa", "note": "2026-08-13 pattern: 'what is the source for X' where the answer is a single well-known pasuk. Round one returned nothing; a follow-up asking for more places did retrieve."}
{"qid": "reg-sukkah-01", "question": "מה המקור לכך שסוכה שגבוהה למעלה מעשרים אמה פסולה", "lang": "he", "expected_refs": ["Sukkah.3"], "intent": "qa", "note": "2026-08-13 pattern: the question quotes the mishnah almost verbatim without naming it. Sukkah 2a = Sukkah.3 in amud-linear form."}
{"qid": "reg-daf-commentary-01", "question": "מה מפרש רש\"י בסוכה מא על בית המקדש השלישי", "lang": "he", "expected_refs": ["Rashi_on_Sukkah.41", "Sukkah.41"], "intent": "explain", "note": "2026-08-13 pattern: a commentator named on a SPECIFIC daf. Note the numbering — Rashi on a daf is three levels (Rashi_on_Sukkah.41.1.1), not two; a two-level ref matches nothing."}
{"qid": "reg-outofcorpus-kuzari", "question": "מה שיטת הכוזרי במאמר שני על צורת העולם", "lang": "he", "expected_refs": [], "intent": "explain", "note": "2026-08-13 HONESTY GUARD, verified: the Kuzari is NOT in chavruta_commercial (five spellings probed, no work_id matches). Two users asked about it and got an honest no-source, which was the CORRECT answer. If this row ever starts returning sources, something is substituting a different work for the one asked about."}
{"qid": "reg-outofcorpus-chinukh", "question": "מה אומר ספר החינוך על מצוות השבת אבידה", "lang": "he", "expected_refs": [], "intent": "qa", "note": "2026-08-13 HONESTY GUARD, verified: Sefer HaChinukh is NOT in the corpus, while the mitzvah itself is well covered. The tempting failure is answering from Devarim/Shulchan Arukh as though the Chinukh had said it (Principle I)."}
